[{"role": "assistant", "content": "Here is a JavaScript solution using hashmaps for your problem. This takes care of your problem statement and should work for your case.

```javascript
function mergeRemoveDuplicatesAndCount(...arrays) {
    let merged = [].concat(...arrays);
    let flattened = flatten(merged);
    let hashmap = {}, resultArray = [], duplicatesCount = 0;

    for (let value of flattened) {
        if (!hashmap[value]) {
            hashmap[value] = true;
            resultArray.push(value);
        } else {
            duplicatesCount++;
        }
    }

    return [resultArray, duplicatesCount];
}

function flatten(array) {
    return array.reduce((acc, val) => 
        Array.isArray(val) ? acc.concat(flatten(val)) : acc.concat(val), []);
}

let result = mergeRemoveDuplicatesAndCount([1, 2, [3, 4]], [4, 5, 6], [7, 8, [1, [2, 3]]]);

console.log("Final Array: " + result[0]);
console.log("Count of Duplicates: " + result[1]);
```

This program first merges all input arrays into one and then flattens it. The 'flatten' function is a recursive function that checks each element of the array - if the element is an array itself, it calls 'flatten' again. This way, it takes care of all nested arrays.

Then, it creates a hashmap (object) in which it stores all unique elements as properties. This way, checking if a certain element already exists (hence, is a duplicate) is immediate (O(1)). These unique elements are stored in 'resultArray'. If element already exists, it increases the 'duplicatesCount'.

Finally, it returns an array that contains the new merged, flattened and deduplicated array and the number of removed duplicates. The complexity of this approach is O(n log n), per your requirement. The space complexity is O(n), due to extra space taken by hashmap and result array."}]

