[{"role": "assistant", "content": "Considering the prerequisites, you should have a basic understanding of Svelte.js, state management, reactivity and observer patterns. The following example demonstrates how to set up a writable store, observing and manipulating it asynchronously. The Svelte store handles a large number of subscribers efficiently so you don't need to worry about performance optimization:

```javascript
// store.js
import { writable } from "svelte/store";

// Custom Greetings Store
const createGreetingsStore = () => {
  const { subscribe, set, update } = writable([]);

  return {
    subscribe,
    addGreeting: (greeting) => update(greetings => [...greetings, greeting]),
  };
};

export const greetings = createGreetingsStore();
```

Now, in a Svelte component (`App.svelte`) you can use this store and observe its changes:

```svelte
<script>
  import { greetings } from "./store.js";

  let newGreeting = "";

  const addGreeting = () => {
    if(newGreeting.length > 0){
      greetings.addGreeting(newGreeting);
      newGreeting = "";
    }
  };

  // Asynchronously observing the store changes
  greetings.subscribe((value) => {
    // If you have a thousand of subscribers, this will run for each of them
    console.log("Store value changed: ", value);
  });

</script>

<input type="text" bind:value={newGreeting} placeholder="Type your greeting here...">
<button on:click={addGreeting}>Add Greeting</button>

<ul>
  {#each $greetings as greeting (greeting)}
    <li>{greeting}</li>
  {/each}
</ul>
```

Now, every time a greeting is added, the `greetings.subscribe` function will run, logging the new value to the console. You can consider this as a form of the observer pattern, since we are tracking changes to the state of our Svelte store.

Asynchronous handling can be achieved within `greetings.subscribe` function. It can be an async function itself or you can call another async function inside it, depending on how you want to handle the changes in data. It's up to your application demands.

Svelte utilizes a reactive model to ensure every subscriber efficiently updates only when there are changes, tackling the need for optimization for a large number of subscribers. This leads to a more efficient handling of a thousand or more subscribers."}]

