[{"role": "assistant", "content": "To complete the function `chooseNum`, you would need to first define a helper function to check if a number is prime, then generate an array from the range `[x, y]`, and finally, pick out and return the largest prime if there are any, otherwise return `-1`. Here's an enhanced code in JavaScript that does that:

```javascript
const chooseNum = (x, y) => {
  // Return -1 if x is greater than y
  if (x > y) {
    return -1;
  }

  // Helper function check if a number is prime
  const isPrime = num => {
    for(let i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(num); i++) {
      if(num % i === 0) return false;
    }
    return num > 1;
  };

  // Construct the array
  const arr = Array.from({length: y-x+1}, (_, i) => x+i);

  // Filter out primes
  const primes = arr.filter(isPrime);

  // Return the largest prime or -1 if there are no primes
  return primes.length ? Math.max(...primes) : -1;
};

console.log(chooseNum(12, 15));  // Outputs: 13
console.log(chooseNum(13, 12));  // Outputs: -1
```

In this code, if `x` is greater than `y`, the function would automatically return `-1` to handle that error case. Also, by using `Array.filter()` and a helper function `isPrime`, this code ensures only prime numbers are selected. If no primes are found, `-1` is returned as expected."}]

