You can use the .done, .fail, and .always methods on this object to handle the results of the Ajax request. 

Here's an example:

```javascript
var xmlRequest = $.post("/url/file/", { 'id' : object.id }, successFunction, 'json');

xmlRequest.done(function(response) {
    console.log(response);
});

xmlRequest.fail(function(jqXHR, textStatus, errorThrown) {
    console.log("Failed with status: " + textStatus + ". Error: " + errorThrown);
});

xmlRequest.always(function() {
    console.log("Request completed.");
});
```

In this case, if the request fails (like if the server returns an error code), the `fail` handler will log the status and error. If the request completes (regardless of whether it was successful or not), the `always` handler will log a message.

These methods are also chainable, so you could also write it like this:

```javascript
$.post("/url/file/", { 'id' : object.id }, null, 'json')
    .done(function(response) {
        console.log(response);
    })
    .fail(function(jqXHR, textStatus, errorThrown) {
        console.log("Failed with status: " + textStatus + ". Error: " + errorThrown);
    })
    .always(function() {
        console.log("Request completed.");
    });
```    
This way, even if the 'successFunction' doesn't get called, you still get some feedback about what's going wrong with the request."}]

