[{"role": "assistant", "content": "Here's a function that uses the Luhn algorithm, commonly used for credit card number validation in JavaScript:

```javascript
function validateCardNumber(number) {
    var regex = new RegExp("^[0-9]{16}$");
    if (!regex.test(number))
        return false;

    return luhnCheck(number);
}

function luhnCheck(val) {
    var sum = 0;
    for (var i = 0; i < val.length; i++) {
        var intVal = parseInt(val.substr(i, 1));
        if (i % 2 == 0) {
            intVal *= 2;
            if (intVal > 9) {
                intVal = 1 + (intVal % 10);
            }
        }
        sum += intVal;
    }
    return (sum % 10) == 0;
}

console.log(validateCardNumber('4532015112830366'));  // Should return true
console.log(validateCardNumber('8273123273520569'));  // Should return false
```

This script first checks if the input matched the regex `^[0-9]{16}$`, which checks if the input is exactly 16 digits long. If not, it returns false. If so, it proceeds to run the Luhn check.

The luhnCheck function goes through each digit and if it's an even position digit (considering the first position as 0), it doubles the digit. If this doubling results in a number greater than 9, it adds the digits of the result (equivalent to subtracting 9). In the end, it checks if the total sum is divisible by 10. If yes, then the credit card number is valid according to the Luhn algorithm. Otherwise, it's not valid. 

Note: This validation only checks if the number is mathematically valid. It does not check if it's a real, active credit card number. For that, you will need to use a service provided by credit card processing companies."}]

